{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "04-determinants.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "Determinants",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Compute determinants, see that the determinant of a product is the product of determinants, and recognise a matrix with no inverse.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Determinant of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix\n> $\\det \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix} = ad - bc$: the signed area of the parallelogram the columns span.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let A = [1, 2; 3, 4]",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "det(A)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null,
      "stepwise": 0
    },
    {
      "src": "det([2, 1, 0; 1, 3, 1; 0, 1, 2])",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Products",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!theorem] Determinant of a product\n> $\\det(AB) = \\det A \\cdot \\det B$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let B = [0, 1; 1, 0]",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "det(A*B)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "det(A)*det(B)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "When the determinant is zero",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!theorem] Invertibility\n> A square matrix has an inverse exactly when its determinant is not zero. A zero determinant means the columns lie on one line (or plane): the area they span is flat.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "det([1, 2; 2, 4])",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "rref([1, 2; 2, 4])",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The reduced form has a row of zeros: the second column is twice the first.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "det([2, 1; 5, 3])",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Compute the determinant.",
      "hints": [
        "$ad - bc$ with $a = 2,\\ b = 1,\\ c = 5,\\ d = 3$."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "det([3, 6; 1, 2])",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Compute the determinant. Is the matrix invertible?",
      "hints": [
        "Is one column a multiple of the other?"
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "det([1, 0, 0; 0, 2, 0; 0, 0, 3])",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Compute the determinant of this diagonal matrix.",
      "hints": [
        "For a diagonal matrix it is the product of the diagonal."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> The determinant is a single number that measures how a matrix scales area; it multiplies across products, and it is zero exactly when the matrix cannot be undone.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
